{"id": "af896dc4-3748-4a2c-892e-6f77cf927781", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:10:22.798702", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "33b3f6bb-b7e7-4860-a0fc-f10db6f9e765", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "So the environment: no code, no datasets, no network, no GPU, python 3.14 without torch. Design-only task per track.md.

The researcher asks for three things: inspection report, proposed approach outline, assumptions/uncertainties. Then end_researcher_turn with stage=plan.

Researcher topics: "neurips2024/93022/6:setup_4", "neurips2024/93022/6:setup_5" — these refer to source documents I don't have access to. I should note that no source documents are available locally; only task.json. I should flag that I cannot verify source-supported setup details and list targeted questions per topic.

Now let me think about the actual content: SiT (Scalable in Diffusion Transformers? Actually SiT = "Diffusion Models Beat GANs on Image Synthesis"? No — SiT here in the question means "Scalable Interpolant Transformers" (Ma et al. 2024, "SiT: Exploring Flow and Diffusion-based Generative Models with Scalable Interpolant Transformers"). But the question's framing "SiT (Scalable in Diffusion Transformers)" with a "time attention mechanism" generating long trajectories — this is more like a trajectory-generation / dynamics-modeling setting. The question mentions "time attention" and generating "extremely long, consistent trajectories" capturing "dynamics over multiple timescales". This resembles the Hyena paper (Poli et al., "Long Range Language Modeling via Gated State-Spaces", "Hyena Hierarchy") where long convolutions replace attention, and also resembles work on "trajectory-level consistency" for physics-based generation.

Actually the NeurIPS 2024 reference (neurips2024/93022) — perhaps a paper on long trajectory generation with diffusion transformers. There's a paper "Diffusion Models as ... " hmm. Perhaps "Physics-Driven ..." Not certain. I should not invent specifics; instead structure the design generically and mark where the source is needed.

Key design elements:

Arms:
- A0: SiT + original time attention (full bidirectional attention over time axis) — baseline
- A1: SiT + non-causal Hyena operator (long conv + gating, no causal mask)
- Controls:
  - A2: SiT + causal Hyena (causal long conv mask) — isolates causality from operator class
  - A3: SiT + parameter/FLOP-matched non-Hyena alternatives: (i) linear attention / low-rank global linear ops, (ii) standard causal temporal attention (mask ablation), (iii) mamba/SSM or gated MLP baseline, (iv) temporal depthwise Conv1d/TCN (short conv) — to check whether gains come from implicit long conv rather than gating
  - A4: truncated-kernel Hyena (kernel length limited to training horizon) vs full extrapolatory kernel at inference — separates "long-range inductive bias" from "length extrapolation"
  - A5: No temporal mixing at all (per-frame MLP + spatial mixing only) — necessity check for a time mixer
  - A6: matched data/noise schedule; identical interpolant/diffusion formulation to avoid confounding formulation with operator.

Metrics:
1. Trajectory-level consistency:
   - Rolling/rollout consistency: given the model generates whole sequences via diffusion, measure agreement between overlapping generations from different conditioning windows (e.g., infill consistency: generate full trajectory vs. generate the same segment conditioned on disjoint contexts; compute disagreement metric, e.g., RMSE or geodesic distance).
   - Cycle/round-trip error; time-reversal consistency for reversible dynamics; conservation-law error (energy/momentum drift) where the dataset has known invariants; constraint violation rate.
   - Autocorrelation of error over time (error growth curve); error half-life / Lyapunov-style divergence exponent vs ground truth; effective horizon (first time t where error exceeds threshold τ).
   - Long-horizon drift: KL/Wasserstein between generated trajectory distribution and data distribution restricted to the final window (does the tail collapse to junk/mode).
   - Repetition/degeneration diagnostics (stationary collapse, entropy collapse, periodic lock), e.g., generated signal variance ratio to data variance at long lags; distinct-mode count.
2. Multi-timescale dynamics capture:
   - Power spectral density error across frequency decades: log-frequency binned spectral distance, spectral kurtosis / low-frequency (slow drift) vs high-frequency (fast oscillation) energy ratio match.
   - Structure function exponent matching (ν_2, ν_4) for multiscale/multifractal processes; wavelet energy per scale error.
   - Autocorrelation function matching at multiple lags / integral timescale error; timescale-specific error (short-lag RMSE, medium-lag RMSE, long-lag RMSE).
   - Koopman/DMD eigenvalue spectrum (decay rates & frequencies) comparison; latent-dynamics linearity.
   - Per-scale log-likelihood / denoising loss decomposition (loss at multiple noise levels as proxy for different timescales — needs justification).
3. Sample quality / coverage:
   - Distribution-level: Fréchet distance on trajectory embeddings (e.g., trained latent or Inception-style for time series — sFID, or VAE-latent Fréchet), MIS (multi-sample matching distance), JS divergence of marginals, MMD with multiple bandwidths / Gaussian kernel MMD.
   - Discriminative score; class/conditional coverage (mode coverage), recall/precision of generated trajectories.
   - Diversity: pairwise distance distribution of samples vs real samples at multiple horizons (avoid deterministic collapse).
   - Standard: per-step RMSE, CRPS, and NLL bounds where available (though likelihood in diffusion needs SDE/flow ODE — possible with flow-matching SiT via ODE log-density, expensive).

Scaling/length axes:
- Train at horizon T_train (e.g., 256 tokens/steps), evaluate at T_eval ∈ {T_train, 2×, 4×, 8×, 16×} (length extrapolation) AND separately train at long T (matched budget-per-token) to separate extrapolation from capacity.
- Compute length-vs-consistency curves for both arms; the question's "extremely long" needs a definition — declare an operational definition: ≥16× training horizon and ≥10^3–10^4 steps, or the horizon over which autoregressive/iterative error growth is bounded.

Controls / rigor:
- Parameter-count, FLOP, and effective-receptive-field matched comparisons. For Hyena vs attention, need to match params (order of gating, number of heads) and report params/FLOPs at each T. Also report wall-clock and memory.
- Kernel-length and depth ablations of Hyena; number of Hyena orders (2 vs 3 gating branches).
- Same training data, same optimizer/schedule/diffusion hyperparams, same seed count ≥3 seeds per arm, report seed variance; paired seed comparisons.
- Data-leakage/normalization parity; identical tokenization.
- Non-causality isolation: causal vs non-causal Hyena at identical everything (only mask differs). Important: in a diffusion-over-whole-trajectory model, temporal non-causality is already bidirectional; the cleanest ablation of causality may be in autoregressive/infilling evaluation. Discuss that the causal Hyena variant makes the model factorize differently and needs consistent evaluation protocol (bidirectional infill vs rollout).
- Attention-length controls: since attention degrades quadratically in cost, at long T the attention arm may need windowed/sliding attention — this confounds operator class with window truncation. Control by including: full-quadratic attention evaluated at long T (memory permitting with gradient checkpointing / smaller model), and windowed attention at matched receptive field. Must be explicit: "attention arm at long T is windowed (same window as Hyena's kernel-support), reported separately from full attention".
- "Oracle" upper bound: train directly on long trajectories (no extrapolation) for both arms at same token budget.
- Negative control: shuffled-phase / time-reversed data to confirm metrics respond to dynamics rather than marginals.

Feasibility:
- Datasets (assumption needed): synthetic physics with known multi-timescale structure — (i) damped/driven harmonic oscillators with a spectrum of frequencies, (ii) N-body gravitational or spring-mass chains (conservation-law metrics), (iii) Lorenz-63/96 & Rössler (chaos, Lyapunov horizon), (iv) Kuramoto or multiscale stochastic processes / fractional Brownian motion & OU mixtures (controlled power-law spectra for timescale tests), (v) real-ish: MoCap walking/AMASS or PMMM motion, or physics videos (Physion/MOVi) tokenized. Recommend a hierarchy: primary = controlled synthetic multi-timescale generators with known ground truth (allows exact conservation & spectral metrics), secondary = physics simulation (N-body), tertiary = real motion capture / video-token trajectories for external validity.
- Model sizes: small SiT-B-like with dimension 256-384, depth 6-12, token = per-timestep latent vector or patch of timesteps. Training budgets: e.g., 50-200k steps for small models. Compute assumption: state as assumption; the environment here has no GPU/torch, so any real run must happen elsewhere.
- Note the environment limitation: 224 CPU cores, no GPU, no network, no torch → design-only here; feasibility estimates are analytical (FLOP formulas) rather than measured.

Analysis plan:
- Pre-registered hypotheses and decision rule: primary endpoint = consistency metric at 16× T_train and 8× T_train, with equivalence/non-inferiority margin; secondary = multi-timescale spectral error; require Hyena non-causal arm to (1) be within X% of attention baseline at T_train, and (2) degrade ≤ Y% as much at 8-16×, (3) beat windowed attention at matched receptive field, (4) causal-vs-non-causal difference attributed to causality.
- Statistical tests: mixed-effects model metric ~ arm × length × seed; multiple comparison correction; report CIs via seed bootstrap; report effect sizes.
