runcated to T_train (isolates long-range inductive bias from extrapolation ability). Same data, tokenizer, interpolant/diffusion formulation, noise schedule, optimizer, step budget, \u22653 seeds per arm; params/FLOPs/wall-clock/peak-memory reported at each evaluated length.\n\n**\"Extremely long, consistent trajectories\" \u2014 concrete metrics.** I would define it operationally, not rhetorically: length \u226516\u00d7 T_train or \u226510^3\u201310^4 steps, plus a *consistency* requirement (bounded error growth), because raw length alone is trivially satisfiable by degenerate output.\n- *Consistency*: error-growth curve and effective horizon (first t where RMSE > \u03c4); overlap/infill consistency (same segment generated under disjoint contexts must agree); conservation-law drift and constraint-violation rate on systems with known invariants; time-reversal/round-trip error; tail-window distributional divergence (does the last 25% of a long rollout collapse to stationary/periodic junk); degeneration diagnostics (variance-ratio vs. data at long lags, autocorrelation entropy, repeat/loop detection).\n- *Multi-timescale*: log-binned PSD distance across frequency decades; integral-timescale and multi-lag ACF error; structure-function exponents (\u03bd\u2082, \u03bd\u2084) / wavelet per-scale energy error for multiscale processes; DMD/Koopman eigenvalue (growth-rate & frequency) matching.\n- *Quality/coverage*: trajectory-embedding Fr\u00e9chet distance and sFID, MMD with multiple bandwidths, multi-sample matching distance, discriminative score, precision/recall & mode coverage vs. real trajectories, plus diversity of pairwise distances at several horizons (catches mode collapse that quality scores reward).\n\n**Length axis.** Evaluate every arm at T \u2208 {T_train, 2\u00d7, 4\u00d7, 8\u00d7, 16\u00d7} in two regimes: pure extrapolation, and *also* a model trained directly at long T under the same token budget (upper bound that separates \"extrapolates\" from \"has capacity\"). Report error-vs-length slopes on log axes \u2014 the slope comparison (attention vs Hyena) is the primary statistical object, more informative than any single-length number.\n\n**Analysis / decision rule (pre-registered).** Primary endpoint: consistency at 8\u00d7 and 16\u00d7 with a non-inferiority margin vs. A0 at T_train; success requires A1 to (a) be within margin of A0 at T_train, (b) degrade materially less at 8\u201316\u00d7, (c) beat windowed-attention A3 at matched receptive field, and (d) show a measurable causal-vs-non-causal gap in A2. Mixed-effects model `metric ~ arm \u00d7 length \u00d7 seed` with correction for multiplicity, seed-bootstrap CIs, effect sizes; negative control on shuffled-phase/time-reversed data to prove metrics respond to dynamics rather than marginals; mechanistic readout of learned Hyena impulse-response support vs. attention range/entropy in A0 to explain the failure mode.\n\n**Feasibility estimate (analytical only).** Small SiT-style model (d\u2248256\u2013384, 6\u201312 blocks), tokens = per-step or short-patch trajectory latents; quadratic-attention cost/memory at 16\u00d7T_train is the binding constraint (memory \u2248 O(L\u00b2\u00b7heads) per layer \u2014 tractable only with gradient checkpointing or reduced model size), whereas Hyena is O(L log L) via FFT. So the design is executable on modest GPU hardware *outside this environment*, and 224 CPUs here suffice only for a tiny CPU-scale pilot of the metric harness, not for training.\n\n## 3) Assumptions and open questions (explicitly not facts)\n\n**Assumptions (must be confirmed):** (A1) \"SiT\" = a diffusion/flow transformer adapted to trajectory generation with a dedicated attention over the *time* axis; note that in the image-domain SiT, attention is over spatial patches and the diffusion timestep enters via adaptive layer-norm, so \"original time attention\" needs source grounding. (A2) Data source: I assume we must choose it \u2014 synthetic controlled multi-timescale generators (oscillator banks with frequency spectra, mixed-OU/fractional-Brownian processes with tunable power-law spectra) as primary, N-body/spring-mass physics as secondary (gives exact conservation metrics), real motion-capture/video-token trajectories as tertiary; none of these is specified by the task. (A3) \"Extremely long\" is *my* operational definition (\u226516\u00d7 T_train), not a source-supported number. (A4) Compute budget assumed modest (single-node multi-GPU, hours-to-days per arm), which caps arms/seeds. (A5) Full-trajectory (non-autoregressive) diffusion generation, so \"non-causal\" is the natural default and causality is introduced only as a mask ablation. (A6) Evaluation protocol details (\u03c4 thresholds, margin values, number of samples) are unspecified and are design choices I would set, not measurements.\n\n**Questions for you (I have a 6-question budget; these two topics are what I need):**\nFor `setup_4`: (Q1) What exactly is the baseline \"time attention\" in the source's SiT \u2014 full bidirectional attention over timestep tokens, or attention over spatial/agent tokens with the diffusion timestep handled by modulation? (Q2) What trajectory data and sequence lengths (T_train, and the \"extremely long\" evaluation length) does the source actually use?\nFor `setup_5`: (Q3) Which Hyena configuration is the source's \u2014 kernel length/decay, gating order, which layers use Hyena vs. MLP, and is the mask truly bidirectional? (Q4) What quality/consistency metrics does the source report, if any (so my primary endpoint is comparable rather than invented)?\nOptional remaining budget: (Q5) is the comparison param-matched or FLOP-matched in the source, and at which model size? (Q6) is generation one-shot over the whole trajectory or windowed/inpainting rollouts?", "evidence": "Direct environment probing (all commands run in /workspace):\n- `ls -la /workspace` -> only `-rw------- task.json` (511 B); `find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -type d`